<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: Collision You Did Not Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        In <a href="7.1.html">7.1</a> you painted a ledge and it held you up. Press Play again and
        stand on the floor, then select the <code>Collision</code> tilemap and read <em>every</em>
        component on it. There are three you did not add, and each one is load-bearing:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Component</th><th>What it contributes</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Tilemap Collider 2D</code></td>
            <td>Turns tiles into physics shapes. It builds <strong>one small collider per solid tile</strong> — the whole cave becomes a few hundred separate shapes.</td>
            <td>Every one of those shapes is a candidate in every physics query. It works, and it is quietly expensive.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Composite Collider 2D</code></td>
            <td>Fuses those separate shapes into <strong>one merged outline</strong>. The tilemap collider feeds it, and it publishes a single clean polygon.</td>
            <td>One more component to understand, and one setting that silently decides whether it is used at all.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Rigidbody 2D</code></td>
            <td>Unity adds this automatically when a composite appears. It is what lets the merged shape be a body the physics engine owns.</td>
            <td>Nothing, if you leave it alone. It must stay <strong>Static</strong> — a floor that moves is a bug in a cave.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Already done for you — do not do it twice.</strong> If you follow an older
        tutorial and add a <strong>second</strong> Tilemap Collider 2D to <code>Collision</code>,
        you do not get twice the colliders — you get a floor with duplicated shapes and bizarre
        edge behaviour you will blame on your movement code. Before you add any component in this
        unit, read the Inspector first. Here is the exact state of the shipped tilemap: the
        tilemap collider's <strong>Composite Operation</strong> is set to <strong>Merge</strong>
        (older documentation calls this checkbox <em>Used By Composite</em>), and the rigidbody's
        <strong>Body Type</strong> is <strong>Static</strong>.
    </div>

    <p>
        So your work this hour is not <em>adding</em> collision. It is three tuning jobs: proving
        the composite is actually doing its job, finishing the list of physics
        <strong>layers</strong> this project shipped without, and separating <em>solid</em> from
        <em>decorative</em> — because right now those two things live under one name.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Reading a Component You Did Not Write</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The tilemap collider builds a shape per tile. The composite merges them. If the merge setting were switched off, how many physics shapes would the cave have, and what evidence would you look for on screen to detect it?</li>
            <li>The rigidbody is Static. What does Static mean about the body's willingness to move, and why is that the correct choice for a cave floor rather than an oversight?</li>
            <li>Standing on this floor, your ground check from <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> succeeds. So does the raycast care whether it hit one merged polygon or four hundred separate tile shapes?</li>
            <li>Seams between tiles are a famous platformer bug — the player snagging on an invisible edge. Which of the three components is the fix for it, and why does merging remove edges that art never showed?</li>
            <li>Both the <code>Collision</code> tilemap and a decorative backdrop can be painted from the same tileset. What is the only thing that should distinguish them, and where would you record that difference — on the tiles, or on the tilemap object?</li>
            <li>Name one thing that should be solid but is <em>not</em> part of this tilemap (a hint: you have been shot at by it since <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>). Why must it be its own object rather than a painted tile?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Read each snippet for what it does in isolation. None of these is from a video game —
        but all of them are about deciding <em>who is allowed to notice whom</em>.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — An airport security lane, deciding what gets scanned:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The belt only asks the scanner about bags whose tag says
// "checked". Every other bag passes the arch untouched.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — A hospital triage desk, filtering by category:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>if (triageMask.Contains(patient.category))
    {
        Debug.Log("Seen by this desk.");
    }</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — A restaurant pass, deciding what is visible to the diner:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>plate.sortingOrder = -1;  // garnish sits behind the dish</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — A one-way valve on a brewery line:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Wort flows down through the valve and cannot flow back up.
// The valve is not a wall - it is a wall with a direction.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 5 — Air traffic control altitude bands (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Two aircraft at the same spot are only "conflicting" if they
// share a band. Different bands, no conflict, no matter how close.</code></pre>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the ground check you already own.</strong> In
        <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> you wrote a <code>LayerMask</code> field and passed it into
        <code>Physics2D.Raycast</code>. Open that script and answer <em>before you touch
