            <li>The tag <code>Ground</code> already exists and is already on the <code>Collision</code> tilemap. If you could fix this with a tag instead of a layer, what would the raycast call look like, and what would it cost you every frame?</li>
            <li>There is a real difference between a <em>tag</em> and a <em>layer</em> in Unity. You have been using tags since Unit 2 and you made exactly one layer in <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a>. State the difference in one sentence each.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Finish the Layer List</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — the discovery.</strong> Open
        <code>Edit → Project Settings → Tags and Layers</code> and read the two lists side by
        side. The <strong>Tags</strong> list holds six names, and two of them are
        <code>Ground</code> and <code>GroundCheck</code>. The <strong>Layers</strong> list is
        where the difference shows: as the project <em>shipped</em> it held Default,
        TransparentFX, Ignore Raycast, Water, UI, a run of empty slots, and three leftover
        placeholders called <code>Layer 1</code>, <code>Layer 2</code> and <code>Layer 3</code> —
        and <strong>no <code>Ground</code> layer at all</strong>. That is exactly why
        <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> had you type one into the first empty user slot. Confirm yours
        is still there before you go any further: every mask in this unit gets pointed at it.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — finish the list.</strong> <code>Ground</code> is the one layer you have,
        and one bucket is not a layer system. Add the ones the rest of this game needs:
        <code>Player</code>, <code>Enemy</code>, <code>Projectile</code> and
        <code>Decoration</code>. Renaming an unused slot is normal and expected.
    </p>
    <p>
        Then go and assign them, object by object: the <code>Collision</code> tilemap becomes
        <code>Ground</code>; the Player prefab becomes <code>Player</code>; the enemy and turret
        prefabs become <code>Enemy</code>; the projectile prefab becomes
        <code>Projectile</code>; and anything you paint that is only scenery becomes
        <code>Decoration</code>. A layer set only at runtime on one instance is worthless —
        <strong>assign it on the prefab</strong>, so every copy inherits it.
    </p>
    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Layer or tag — they are not the same tool, and this project shipped with only
        one of them.</strong> The <code>Collision</code> tilemap carries the <em>tag</em>
        <code>Ground</code>, and that tag is what your <code>CompareTag</code> checks read. But a
        raycast mask cannot use a tag — it needs a <em>layer</em>, and the project you were
        handed had none. Here is the part that catches people, so read it twice: a mask naming a
        layer that does not exist does <strong>not</strong> quietly widen to everything.
        <code>LayerMask.GetMask("Ground")</code> in a project with no <code>Ground</code> layer
        logs an error and hands back a mask with <em>no bits set</em>. An empty mask is not a
        wide mask — the ray is allowed to notice nothing, the ground check reads false on a floor
        you can see, and the player jumps in mid-air forever. An Inspector dropdown left alone is
        the same silence by a different route: Unity draws an unset <code>LayerMask</code> as
        <strong>Nothing</strong>. Prove it to yourself once, on purpose, by renaming the layer
        and watching the check stop working. Tags are for <em>identifying</em> one object; layers
        are for <em>filtering</em> whole categories in a query. You will use both, for the rest
        of the year, and mixing them up produces bugs that fail silently.
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — fix the mask, then try to break it.</strong> Point the 6.2 mask at
        <code>Ground</code> and nothing else. Then test the claim: stand next to an enemy and ask
        the ground check whether you are grounded. Before your fix, what did it say? After?
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 4 — the collision matrix.</strong> In
        <code>Project Settings → Physics 2D</code>, find the layer collision matrix — the grid of
        tick-boxes. Decide, layer pair by layer pair, who collides with whom. Then justify two
        specific ticks: should <code>Enemy</code> collide with <code>Enemy</code>? Should
        <code>Projectile</code> collide with <code>Decoration</code>?
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: sorting, the other kind of layer.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Open <code>Project Settings → Tags and Layers</code> again and read the <strong>Sorting Layers</strong> list. It already contains <code>Bottom</code>, <code>Player</code> and <code>Top</code> — and also <code>Layer 2</code> and <code>Layer 4</code>, which are placeholder names nobody ever replaced. Read them as they are rather than as they should be. What is a sorting layer for, and which of the two lists answers "who draws in front"?</li>
            <li>Which list answers "who is allowed to collide"? Write both answers down side by side, because confusing them costs every beginner exactly one evening.</li>
            <li>Your backdrop, your ground, your player and your attack swing all need an order. Assign sorting layers to each, and say why the swing effect needs its own layer above <code>Player</code>.</li>
            <li>Two sprites on the <em>same</em> sorting layer still need an order. What is that second knob called, and where does it live?</li>
            <li>A pillar the player walks <em>behind</em> — which layer does it go on, and does it need a collider at all?</li>
            <li>Is a sorting layer a physics object in any way? Can a change to sorting ever stop the player falling through the floor? Prove your answer from the two lists.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Painting decoration onto the solid tilemap is the trap this whole lesson exists to close.</strong>
        A pebble, a vine, a distant rock — all of them look correct painted on
        <code>Collision</code>, and all of them become rock. Then the thing standing on the
        pebble is you. There are two defensible fixes and you must choose one and defend it:
        paint decoration onto the <code>No Collision</code> tilemap (simple, and it can never
        lie to you), or keep it on a solid tilemap and give that tilemap a
        <code>Decoration</code> layer your queries exclude (more flexible, more to remember).
        Pick one. Write down why.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: One-Way Platforms</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        You want a ledge you can jump <em>up through</em> and then stand on. That is not a
        layer's job and not a sorting layer's job — it is a collider property with a
        <strong>direction</strong>. There are three honest ways to build it, and they are not
        equivalent:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it works</th><th>What it costs</th><th>When it wins</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A separate one-way tilemap</strong> with its own Tilemap Collider 2D + Composite Collider 2D + <code>Platform Effector 2D</code>, and <em>Used By Effector</em> ticked on the composite</td>
            <td>The whole tilemap becomes a membrane you pass upward and stand on downward.</td>
            <td>A third tilemap to maintain, and the direction applies to every tile on it.</td>
            <td>When you want many one-way ledges and they all behave the same.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Per-tile effector</strong> — <code>Platform Effector 2D</code> plus <em>Used By Effector</em> on the tilemap collider</td>
            <td>Individual tiles in the existing tilemap gain a direction.</td>
            <td>It fights the composite merge, and you now have two kinds of tile on one grid.</td>
            <td>Rarely. Know it exists; do not reach for it first.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A loose object</strong> — a plain GameObject with a Box Collider 2D + <code>Platform Effector 2D</code>, placed over a gap in the tiles</td>
            <td>One platform, one object, zero tilemap surgery.</td>
            <td>The platform is no longer part of the painted level, so it can drift out of alignment with the grid.</td>
            <td>When you need exactly one or two, and you need them now.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Whatever you pick, the effector has a <strong>Surface Arc</strong> and an
        <strong>Offset</strong> that decide which direction counts as "up". Predict what happens
        when the arc is rotated 90°, and then try it on a wall.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Choose, Then Justify</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Which of the three approaches did you build, and what in your level made it the right one?</li>
            <li>A one-way platform that is one tile thick: what happens to the player who is <em>inside</em> it when the effector is added mid-game? Predict before testing.</li>
            <li>If a one-way ledge sits on a layer excluded from the player's mask, does the player still stand on it? Which system decides that — the layer mask or the effector?</li>
            <li>A one-way platform is a wall with a direction. Name one other object in this project that would improve if it had a direction (the ladder in <a href="7.3.html">7.3</a> is a clue).</li>
            <li>Your ground raycast fires straight down. Does a one-way platform answer it? What does that mean for the player standing on one and trying to jump?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Bug Hunt: Symptoms With Two Possible Causes</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Your player draws <em>behind</em> a wall. Is that a physics layer problem or a sorting problem? State which list you would open first, and what you would change.</li>
            <li>Your ground raycast reports grounded while you stand beside a turret. Which setting is too wide, and which two of the five layers you just made would have prevented it?</li>
            <li>Set your enemy prefab to the <code>Ground</code> layer by accident. Your ground check now reports grounded whenever the enemy is under your feet. Explain why that happened using only the words <em>mask</em> and <em>layer</em> — and say what else in the game quietly started colliding differently.</li>
            <li>Paint decorative rocks onto the <code>Collision</code> tilemap and walk into one. Which of the two fixes from the trap callout is the cheaper one here, and which is the more flexible one?</li>
            <li>Untick <em>Used By Effector</em> (or clear <strong>Composite Operation</strong> back to <strong>None</strong>) on the one-way tilemap. Does the player still pass through upward? What did that single setting control?</li>
            <li>Your projectile hits the turret that fired it and the turret takes damage from itself. Before you solve it with layers, find out how the projectile leaves the barrel today. Which fix is already half-present in your build — layers, or spawn position?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Depth and Direction</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Build a foreground pillar on the <code>Top</code> sorting layer with a solid collider, and walk behind it. What classic game moment did you just build, and why does it need <em>both</em> a sorting layer and a collider?</li>
            <li>Turn <strong>Composite Operation</strong> back to <strong>None</strong> on the tilemap collider and run along a long floor at speed. Can you feel a difference? Then put it back to <strong>Merge</strong> — did anything else in the game change, or only the feel?</li>
            <li>Add a <em>second</em> Tilemap Collider 2D to <code>Collision</code> and press Play. Describe what the Inspector now shows and what the floor does. Remove it. What rule about inherited components did you just learn the hard way?</li>
            <li>Write the one-sentence version of a trade-off you made today, in the shape of a Create PT response: what resource did you spend, and what did you save? (Merging a few hundred shapes into one is a good candidate.)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Name the three components already on <code>Collision</code> and say what each does</li>
            <li>Explain what a composite fixes, and name the setting that switches it on</li>
            <li>Say out loud why <code>Ground</code> being a tag was not enough, and extend the physics layers</li>
            <li>Explain the difference between a physics layer and a sorting layer, with one use of each</li>
            <li>Fix your 6.2 ground-check mask and prove the fix with a test</li>
            <li>Defend your choice of one-way platform approach</li>
            <li>Break this feature into parts small enough to look each one up on its own, and say in one line what each part does</li>
            <li>Say which earlier lesson each part of this feature came from, and where you would reread it</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

